Subject: Re: Handoff: EdgeWave integration — traffic sentry & SDK fallbacks

Samir / EdgeWave team — thanks for the materials, Monica. I'm Rishi from the Implementation team; I'll be the main technical point for the integration and cutover. Two quick asks to help finalize our plan:
1) Can you confirm which environment will handle the July 12 soft launch (staging or production namespace)?
2) Share any expected long-running prompts or very large context lengths (tokens > 8k) so we can pre-validate memory/kv-cache sizing.

Implementation notes / initial recommendations (to be refined after their answers):
- SDK setup: we recommend they start with the hosted SDK (Node) snippet in the attachment, then swap the base_url for the Dedicated endpoint at cutover. Example node snippet we suggest they use (simple retry + jitter):

const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const MAX_RETRY = 5;

async function callRedwood(payload) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (true) {
    attempt++;
    try {
      const res = await fetch(process.env.REDWOOD_URL + '/v1/generate', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_KEY}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify(payload),
        timeout: 120000 // 120s per request timeout recommendation for long prompts
      });

      if (res.status === 429 || res.status === 503) {
        if (attempt >= MAX_RETRY) throw new Error('max retries');
        // read retry-after when provided, otherwise exponential backoff + jitter
        const ra = res.headers.get('retry-after');
        const base = ra ? parseInt(ra, 10) * 1000 : Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, attempt), 30000);
        const jitter = Math.floor(Math.random() * 250);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, base + jitter));
        continue;
      }

      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
      return await res.json();
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt >= MAX_RETRY) throw err;
      const backoff = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, attempt), 30000);
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff));
    }
  }
}

- Rate limits: during the ramp we recommend a per-instance concurrency cap of 8 in Node and 12 in Go workers, plus a token-based leaky-bucket client-side limiter. In practice: throttle to 200 requests/sec per API key during initial 24h, monitor x-rate-limit-remaining headers and scale.
- Timeouts: set request-level timeout = 120s for generation endpoints; for short-summary endpoints use 30s.
- Migration pattern: DNS-style cutover using traffic split (70/30 -> 90/10 -> 100/0) across 2–4 hours with a 30-minute observation between shifts. Implement automatic fallback to hosted model variant on 5xx/503s.

I'll prepare a one-page runbook with the above as action items and a small checklist for sandbox verification.

